1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:10,699
This is the lab oracle, the lab's shared memory. It answers one question: what has the lab agreed on, and who said so?

2
00:00:11,099 --> 00:00:20,000
It holds the findings the whole lab has agreed on, and for each one it names who published it and when.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:30,100
Findings arrive when a member publishes them from their own oracle. Everybody reads it; new entries arrive only by publishing.

4
00:00:30,499 --> 00:00:41,000
Your own oracle holds your working notes; the lab oracle holds what the lab has agreed. It always says which memory an answer comes from.

5
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:54,299
The bookworm finds papers and the blacksmith runs analyses. If the lab has no entry on a topic, it says so, and suggests you check your own oracle.

6
00:00:54,699 --> 00:01:06,000
A real run, on a demo lab memory. The member asks what the lab agreed on for a quality threshold in single-cell experiments, and who published it.

7
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:15,700
It finds one entry: the threshold, who published it, the lab meeting that decided it, and the value used before.

8
00:01:16,099 --> 00:01:35,799
Then the member asks it to change the number. It answers that changes arrive by publishing, shows what the entry says now, and leaves it as it is: a member writes the change in their own oracle and publishes it, once the lab has decided.

9
00:01:36,199 --> 00:01:51,099
To use it, just ask Claude Code: ask the lab oracle what the lab has agreed on about a topic. To add something, ask your own oracle to prepare it, and publish it to the lab.

10
00:01:51,099 --> 00:02:02,899
The first line of the reply is the verdict: Found, Not found or Unsure, with the name of who published the entry.
